invariant: INV-43
file: p256.c
catches: test/widemul-builds.sh
reason: p256.c chooses its arm on a define no build passes, so a host
  object compiles the 32-bit arithmetic and never calls
  p256_wide_verify.c. Every verdict stays right and every test of the
  verifier passes, at the device object's speed. test/widemul-builds.sh
  compiles the file as a host object does and requires its call into
  p256_wide_verify.c. The file picks its arm in one place, so this edit
  compiles under every compiler: with a second test of the define at
  the entry, gcc 13 compiled the entry's call without its declaration
  and the object still called the wide verifier.

--- old
#ifdef CH_CPU_RUNTIME

#include "p256_wide_verify.h"
--- new
#ifdef CH_CPU_HOST

#include "p256_wide_verify.h"
